    "product_id": "b8642716-11b3-4514-8511-688a763b2943",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 021723\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1023 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 18z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and mostly sunny skies today, but flooding of area creeks,\n  streams, and rivers will continue today, following the rain over\n  the past few days.\n\n- Flooding in some of the major waterways like the Salt River may\n  persist through the week.\n \n- Dry and seasonal weather conditions are expected through the\n  next seven days, with lower desert high temperatures in the\n  seventies to around eighty.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \n\nWater vapor satellite and surface observations early this morning\nshowed dry air was quickly spreading across the region, as the\nupper level trough was ejecting eastward through the Four Corners.\nRain showers were also finally coming to an end across south-\ncentral AZ. A lot of water was transported in and squeezed out of\nthe atmosphere over the past 3 days. Latest 3-day measured\nrainfall and radar estimates show a large area east and northeast\nof Phoenix, in the higher terrain, received 5-7 inches and a few\nspots reached 8 inches. Even though the has ended there is still \na lot of runoff moving through the water systems across AZ. \nSeveral USGS and flood control streamflow gauges early this \nmorning were still above designated action/alarm stages and some \nwere in minor to moderate flood stage. Many areal and river flood \nwarnings were still in effect and will remain in effect through \nmost of this morning. Unlike Winter and Spring snowmelt runoff\nfollowing a rain event, runoff following this rain event should \nsubside more quickly. Still, runoff in some of the major waterways\nand watersheds, like the Salt and Verde systems, will take \nlonger to subside. Possibly even most of the week. Salt River \nProject this morning around 3 AM MST was still releasing a very \nhigh volume of water from Bartlett Dam into the Verde River, over \n60000 cfs, and from Granite Reef Dam into the Salt River, over \n34000 cfs. Impacts on the Salt River downstream of Tempe Town Lake\nwill likely develop today as runoff continues. Recreating in the \nVerde and Salt rivers will be very dangerous and is strongly \ndiscouraged. There have been multiple fatalities from recreation \nwith similar flows in the past. Continue to heed all road closures\nas will and do not attempt to cross flooded roads.\n\nBesides the lingering flooding, it will be a very pleasant Fall\nday, with high temperatures this afternoon forecast to reach the\nlow to middle 70s under mostly sunny skies. Winds will be light\nacross south-central AZ and there will be some northerly breezes\nup to 15-25 mph along the lower Colorado River Valley this \nafternoon. Dry conditions will continue Tuesday and Wednesday and\ntemperatures will gradually warm. A shortwave ridge will pass \nover the area tomorrow between the departing upper level trough\nand another Pacific low. Model guidance keeps this next Pacific\nlow on a west-east trajectory through UT and CO on Wednesday. Just\nabout all this transient low will do to the local area is suppress\nheights and keep temperatures from warming more quickly. High\ntemperatures Wednesday will be a bit closer to seasonal levels,\nbut still ~5 degrees below normal, with lower deserts reaching the\nmiddle 70s to near 80 degrees. Morning temperatures the next few\ndays will be seasonally cool, mostly in the 50s across the lower\ndeserts and 40s in higher elevation communities and some of the\ntypically cooler lower desert valley spots.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \n\nThe long term forecast remains dry and seasonal. There is now\nmuch better confidence in the late week and weekend forecast,\nwhich favors ridging building in across the West and a weak cutoff\nlow forming over the Pacific west of Baja California. Weak\ntroughing looks to drag across the local area Friday into Saturday\nahead of the building ridge. Previous forecasts supported slight \nrain chances with this trough, but this trough now looks\ncompletely dry and latest NBM PoPs are 5% or less across the\nregion. These are also the only PoPs in the 7-day forecast. Plenty\nof time to dry out fallowing all the rain. Temperatures during \nthe second half of the week will remain steady near to slightly \nbelow normal and then heading into the weekend temperatures are \nlikely to trend warmer as the ridging builds into the west. Most \nlower deserts may see high temperatures return to the low and \npossibly even mid 80s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1720Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; \nNo aviation weather issues are expected throughout the TAF \nperiod. The overall wind pattern will follow the more typical \ndiurnal tendencies with westerly winds persisting through early \nthis evening before shifting out of the east during the late \nevening hours with speeds generally aob 6 kts. Extended periods \nof light and variable to even calm conditions can be expected. FEW\ncumulus clouds with decks aoa 6 kft can be expected this \nafternoon with some high cirrus cloud decks moving in during the \novernight period. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather issues are expected throughout the TAF \nperiod. Winds will generally prevail out of the west to northwest \nat KIPL and out of the north at KBLH with speeds aob 10 kts, \nalthough some occasional gusts in the mid to upper teens will be \npossible at KBLH this afternoon. Clear skies will prevail through \nthis afternoon before some high cirrus cloud decks move in by \nearly this evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nLingering floodwater runoff in area creeks, streams, and rivers is\nlikely to continue through at least today. Otherwise, rain has\nended and dry seasonal conditions can be expected through the \nnext 7 days. Saturated grounds will help keep surface humidity \nlevels somewhat elevated. Afternoon minimum RHs today will be \naround 50-60% across AZ and 25-35% across southeast CA. Despite \ngradual drying through the week, the minimum RHs will only fall to\naround 35-45% across AZ and 20-30% across southeast CA. Overnight\nrecoveries will remain good through the week, with around 60-80% \nmax RHs most nights. Winds through the next 7 days will mostly \nfollow typical diurnal trends with light speeds and modest \nafternoon upslope gusts. Some breezier north winds may develop \nalong the lower Colorado River Valley at times, but with only \nminor gusts up to 20-25 mph forecast at this time.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict\nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict\n",
